1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,332
Hi, I'm Peter Qin from Carnegie Mellon. This is K2 Cascade, built at HackCMU

2
00:00:05,332 --> 00:00:10,214
on the K2 Horizon family. Today, every agent step goes to the biggest model,

3
00:00:10,214 --> 00:00:15,353
because no model can tell when a step is beyond it. We think the next capability

4
00:00:15,353 --> 00:00:17,730
is a model that knows its own limits.

5
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:22,957
So we asked a concrete question. In a coding agent, which steps actually

6
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:27,125
need a three hundred and seventy five billion parameter model? We built

7
00:00:27,125 --> 00:00:31,470
a ladder. The point nine B and the three point seven B run on this laptop,

8
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:34,640
and the three seventy five B runs through the IFM API.

9
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:40,386
K2 Horizon makes this measurable. Six sizes share one training recipe, one

10
00:00:40,386 --> 00:00:45,131
chat template and one tool call format, so model size is close to the only

11
00:00:45,131 --> 00:00:49,684
variable. Each step goes to the smallest model first. A verifier checks

12
00:00:49,684 --> 00:00:52,570
it, and on failure, the next size takes over.

13
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,554
Here is a live run on this laptop, sped up. The point nine B runs the tests,

14
00:00:58,554 --> 00:01:03,006
then goes looking for the source, at a path it made up. The judge rejects

15
00:01:03,006 --> 00:01:07,457
its next move twice, and the three point seven B takes over. It reads the

16
00:01:07,457 --> 00:01:11,970
file, writes the fix, and runs the tests. They pass, and control goes back

17
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:14,470
to the point nine B to write the summary.

18
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:22,341
Across four tasks and thirty seven runs, ninety percent of the accepted

19
00:01:22,341 --> 00:01:26,687
agent steps ran on the laptop. And different tasks needed different sizes.

20
00:01:26,687 --> 00:01:31,034
One the point nine B solved alone, one needed the three point seven B, and

21
00:01:31,034 --> 00:01:33,560
one really needed the three seventy five B.

22
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,670
When we scored the big model's steps token by token with the three point

23
00:01:38,670 --> 00:01:41,980
seven B, its edge sat in its prose, not in its tool calls.

24
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:47,226
So we asked whether the small model already knows. Before it writes anything,

25
00:01:47,226 --> 00:01:51,141
we read its hidden state, and fit a linear probe to predict whether the

26
00:01:51,141 --> 00:01:55,111
step will be rejected. On the three point seven B it reaches point eight

27
00:01:55,111 --> 00:01:59,026
eight, and it adds real information beyond simple bookkeeping, like the

28
00:01:59,026 --> 00:02:00,790
step count and earlier failures.

29
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:06,038
That signal is exactly what models never pass to each other. They hand off

30
00:02:06,038 --> 00:02:10,286
in English, or copy a cache that carries what the sender computed, but not

31
00:02:10,286 --> 00:02:14,534
how sure it was. The three point seven B and the seven B share a key value

32
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:18,667
layout and a tokenizer. So our next step is to pass that state directly,

33
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:21,250
with the uncertainty travelling alongside it.

34
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:27,548
Thank you to IFM for opening the whole ladder. The code, traces and results

35
00:02:27,548 --> 00:02:31,080
are on GitHub, at WuKong AI CMU, slash K2 Cascade.
